## Causal identification in this design {#we-cannot-identify-a-causal-effect-here .smaller}

::: {.small}
An observational panel of 27 countries, with no assignment mechanism, feedback between the variables and a decade of shared shocks.

Nothing in this design licenses "unemployment **causes** a shift in EU framing".
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
So why draw a causal graph at all?
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Because the graph still does three jobs that do not need identification:

- It says **which association** you are after – for instance the total effect, rather than whatever the software returns
- It tells you **which variables belong in the model**, and, just as usefully, which do not
- It makes your assumptions **legible and arguable** – a reader can disagree with an arrow, and that is a productive disagreement
:::

::: notes
The template speaks of an expected change in framing for a difference of one percentage point in unemployment, which sounds causal, so I want to be plain about what this design can deliver. It is an observational panel of twenty-seven countries, in which nobody assigned unemployment, the variables plausibly feed back into each other, and the whole decade carries shocks that hit every country at once, so nothing in it licenses the sentence "unemployment causes a shift in EU framing". [advance] The question that follows is why we draw a causal graph at all. [advance] The graph does three jobs that need no identification: it says which association you are after, the total effect, for instance, rather than whatever the software returns; it tells you which variables belong in your model and which do not; and it makes your assumptions explicit, so that a reader can disagree with a specific arrow, which I think is a more productive argument than one about a coefficient.
:::

