::: {.fragment .small}
An exclusion is a claim about **who the finding is meant to be about**, and so it is a question about the estimand. The workshop shows these samples beside the curve, as displays of how the association varies, rather than on it.
:::

::: notes
The curve uses all twenty-seven countries over the whole decade, and each restriction on the slide answers a different question, so the restrictions sit beside the curve as displays. Splitting the decade changes the population that the claim is about, since the earlier window contains no crisis and the later one loses the comparison with the years before it. [advance] In both halves the slope of the reproduced specification is steeper than on the full panel, with a lower CR2 p in the crisis years. In the eight countries under an assistance programme the slope is negative, with a CR2 p of about point zero two on fewer than five degrees of freedom, and in the other nineteen it is slightly positive and far from significant. [advance] An exclusion is a claim about whom the finding is meant to describe, which makes it a question about the estimand.
:::

## Weights {#weights-whose-country-year-counts-more .smaller .deepdive run="methods"}

::: {.small}
Each cell is a mean of very different numbers of respondents. Weighting by `n_cy` makes a cell built from 2,000 people count for more than one built from 500.

- **Unweighted** – every country-year is one observation. You are studying *countries*
- **Weighted by `n_cy`** – cells count in proportion to the people behind them. You are closer to studying *people*
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
That changes the estimand, so whose average counts is one of the seven decisions about the question. The curve counts every country-year once. Weighted by respondents, 64.9% of its twins lie below zero with CR2 p < 0.05, and weighted by adult population 24.9%, against 77.2% on the curve.
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
The population weight answers another question, about the average European rather than the average member state.
:::

::: notes
Each cell of the panel is a mean over a very different number of respondents, so weighting by that count makes a country-year built from two thousand people count for more than one built from five hundred. Unweighted, every country-year is one observation and you study countries, with Malta counting as much as Germany, whereas weighted, cells count in proportion to the people behind them, which moves you towards studying people. That is a change of estimand, which is why the workshop treats the weighting as a decision about the question rather than as an argument of the fitting function. [advance] The curve counts every country-year once, because the claim is about countries. Weighted by respondents, the share of specifications that clear the CR2 test falls from about seventy-seven per cent to about sixty-five, and weighted by adult population to a quarter. [advance] The population weight suits a different question, about the average European rather than the average member state.
:::

## Making the axes comparable {.smaller .deepdive run="methods"}

::: {.small}
A coefficient on a share and one on a count, or one from a model of country-years and one from a model of respondents, are not on the same scale. The robustness study and this workshop put them on one scale in three steps:

- Take the test statistic and the degrees of freedom of its fit
- Convert to a **partial correlation** *r* – the association between predictor and outcome, net of everything else in the model, on a fixed −1 to +1 scale
- Apply **claim alignment**, so the sign means the same thing everywhere
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
The df must count the units that carry the evidence. Fed the clustered *t* of the reproduced specification on 26 df, the formula returns *r* = −0.413 in place of −0.242 with the estimate unchanged, so the curve, and every class submission, takes its height from the conventional fit and its class from the CR2 test.
:::

::: notes
The conversion puts every result on the class chart. A coefficient on a share and one on a count, or one from a model of respondents and one from a model of country-years, are not on the same scale, so an axis that holds both would mean nothing. The robustness study, and the workshop after it, take the test statistic and its degrees of freedom, convert them to a partial correlation, and apply claim alignment, so that the sign means the same thing everywhere. [advance] The conversion is only as good as its degrees of freedom. Fed the clustered t of my specification on twenty-six degrees of freedom, it returns minus point four one where the conventional fit gives minus point two four, a larger number from weaker evidence. The curve and your submissions therefore take their height from the conventional fit and their class from the CR2 test, and `submit_exploration()` and `report_result()` send that pair for you.
:::

